Sure! Here's a simple HTML and JavaScript example to create a colorful and interactive joke website:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>My Joke Website</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }
        .container {
            text-align: center;
            background-color: #fff;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
        }
        h1 {
            color: #333;
        }
        .joke {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            color: #555;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }
        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 1em;
            color: #fff;
            background-color: #ff6347;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
            cursor: pointer;
            transition: background-color 0.3s;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #e6553b;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>My Joke Website</h1>
        <div class="joke">
            <p>[Funny Joke]</p>
            <button onclick="revealPunchline()">Push to reveal punchline!</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        function revealPunchline() {
            document.querySelector('.joke').innerHTML = `
                <p>Why don't scientists trust atoms?</p>
                <p>Because they make up everything!</p>
            `;
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple, interactive website with a joke that is revealed when the button is clicked. The CSS styles make it colorful and centered. You can replace the joke text with any real joke you like.
